  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nAn upper level trough is currently over the northeastern CONUS with \nupper ridging over the western two-thirds of the US, keeping \nnorthwesterly flow aloft over the northern Plains into the Upper\nMississippi Valley. At the surface, a low sits over western \nNebraska with surface troughing extending down into the southern\nPlains. In our area, light southerly winds continue with well \nabove normal temperatures as highs rise into the upper 80s \nacross the area. As we move into late Friday afternoon, an upper\ntrough will start to dig into the Pacific Northwest. As this \ntrough continues to dig into the western CONUS into the weekend,\nlee troughing will increase late Friday evening. This will \nstrengthen the pressure gradient across our area Saturday \nthrough Monday, resulting in breezy southerly winds. Sustained \nwinds will be between 15-25 mph with gusts up to 35 mph. As \nabove normal 850 mb temperatures persist, well above normal \nhighs will also continue into early next week with highs in the \nupper 80s. \n\nA pattern change is likely mid next week as the aforementioned upper \ntrough over the western CONUS propagates east into the Rockies on \nWednesday morning. This trough will likely bring the next round of \nrain chances to the area along with more seasonable temperatures. \nHighs look to return to the 70s into the low 80s across the area \nlate next week. Rain chances will start to increase on Wednesday and \ncontinue into Thursday, though details on timing and exact location \nare still uncertain at this time, stay tuned!",
